  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each order is separately identifiable and costed, which is the definition of a job.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A batch is a group of identical units, costed as a group and divided by the good units.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cement is produced in a continuous stream through successive processes.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Advertising is a period cost of the whole firm and cannot be traced to one job.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The job is priced before the year's actual overhead is known, so an absorption rate is used.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    30,000 ÷ 480 good units = ₦62.50; the rejected units carry their share of the cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The whole batch cost is borne by the units that can be sold, so each good unit costs more.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The debit side collects the cost put into the process.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is expected, unavoidable and allowed for in the cost per unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The firm recovers that money, so it reduces the net cost to be spread over the good units.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cost of the process is carried by the units that survive it.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Expected output 750 units; 6,000 ÷ 750 = ₦8.00.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Scrap value 250 × 4 = 1,000; (7,000 − 1,000) ÷ 750 = ₦8.00.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Expected output 750 less actual 700 = 50 units at ₦8 each = ₦400.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The process gains units it did not pay for, so it is debited there and credited to profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The units lost and transferred must equal the units put in, and the values must balance as well.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300 units 40 per cent complete represent 120 equivalent units of work.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    400 × 25% = 100 equivalent units.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The output of one process becomes an input, and is treated as a material in the next.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is not finished, so it is removed from this period's cost and carried forward.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Scrap is a recovery of cost, so it reduces the cost of the process.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fewer good units carry the same cost, so each bears more.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The abnormal figures arise only from the difference between actual and expected output.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Contracts are large, long-term and on site, with work certified and retention money.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each client's work is a separate job, not a continuous stream of identical units.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is valued on the same basis as the good units, which is why it can be written off precisely.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a saving, so it improves the profit of the period.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    12,000 ÷ 2,000 = ₦6.00, and with no loss the output equals the input.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Expectation is the whole test: a normal loss is allowed for in advance, an abnormal loss is not.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The work content is expressed in equivalent units and priced at the process rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Total cost 40,000 + 8,000 = 48,000; price 48,000 × 1.10 = ₦52,800.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The requisition identifies the job and the quantity, and the cost is posted to the job card.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Input units must reappear as good output, losses and work in progress.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only the work done this year on those units is charged to this year.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Identical units are produced in defined groups, which is batch costing.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is nothing to recover, so the cost is spread over the units that survive.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is valued at the good-unit cost and written off, not absorbed into the product.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Opening work in progress is part of this year's cost; closing work in progress is carried forward.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Where units are identical, an average cost per unit is both possible and sufficient.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The scrap value is recovered and deducted; the remainder is spread over the units that survive.
  ]

]

